# Default OpenAI API Configuration
# Copy this file to .env and set your actual values

# LLM Provider Configuration
# Supported providers: openai, anthropic, openrouter, ollama
LLM_PROVIDER=openai

# OpenAI Configuration (when LLM_PROVIDER=openai)
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_MODEL_NAME=gpt-5-2025-08-07

# Anthropic Configuration (when LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic)
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_MODEL_NAME=claude-sonnet-4-5

# OpenRouter Configuration (when LLM_PROVIDER=openrouter)
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
OPENROUTER_MODEL_NAME=google/gemini-2.5-pro
OPENROUTER_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1

# Ollama Configuration (when LLM_PROVIDER=ollama)
# Vision-capable models: qwen2.5vl:7b (recommended), gemma3:4b, llama3.2-vision, llava
# NOTE: qwen2.5vl may struggle with circulation vs libration distinction
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434
# OLLAMA_MODEL_NAME=qwen2.5vl:7b
OLLAMA_MODEL_NAME=gemma3:4b

# Ollama-specific prompt template (optimized for visual pattern recognition)
OLLAMA_PROMPT_TEMPLATE="Look at the resonant‑angle plot (y=0–2π).
If all points stay inside one narrow band (<0.5 of the full height) or inside two opposite narrow horizontal bands with an empty gap between them, answer “resonant”.
If the points span most of the height—climbing in slanted stripes or scattered like pepper across the full y‑range—answer “non‑resonant”.
Ignore a few stray outliers and reply with exactly that single lowercase word.

Respond with JSON format:
{\"status\": \"resonant\" or \"non-resonant\", \"subtype\": \"regular libration\" or \"apocentric libration\" or \"circulation\", \"confidence\": 0.8, \"explanation\": \"brief description of what you see\"}"

# Optional: Custom prompt template for all providers
# 2025-10-30 
# Google Gemini v1
# v1 adjusted
#PROMPT_TEMPLATE="You are an expert astronomer specializing in the analysis of orbital dynamics from scatter plots. Your task is to classify a given plot of a resonant angle versus time into one of three categories: `resonant`, `non-resonant`, or `transient`.\n\nYou MUST follow a strict three-step reasoning process and format your output as a single JSON object.\n\n### STEP 1: DESCRIPTION\nObjectively describe the primary visual characteristics of the entire plot from left to right. Note the overall shape, bounds, and texture of the data points. Do not attempt to classify the plot in this step.\n\n### STEP 2: ANALYSIS\nAnalyze the features you described in Step 1 based on the definitions below. Explicitly state how the visual evidence supports your reasoning.\n\n### STEP 3: CLASSIFICATION\nBased on your analysis, provide the final classification.\n\n---\n\n### CORE DEFINITIONS\n\n1.  **resonant**: The data points are confined within clear horizontal boundaries for the entire duration of the plot. The pattern of oscillation is stable and maintained from left to right, even if it is noisy or has a large amplitude. The key feature is that the motion is **bounded throughout**.\n    *   This includes single sine-wave-like patterns, two distinct horizontal bands (apocentric), or noisy but contained bands.\n\n2.  **non-resonant**: The data points are not confined within clear horizontal boundaries. The motion is unbounded, characterized by either:\n    *   Chaotic scatter that fills most or all of the vertical space (y-axis from 0 to 2π).\n    *   Clear diagonal lines that \"wrap around\" from the top of the plot to the bottom.\n    *   The key feature is that the motion is **unbounded throughout**.\n\n3.  **transient**: The plot shows a clear and distinct change from one behavior type to another. The pattern on the left side of the plot is fundamentally different from the pattern on the right side.\n    *   Examples: The plot begins as `resonant` (bounded) and becomes `non-resonant` (unbounded), or vice-versa.\n    *   The key feature is a **clear transition** between a bounded and an unbounded state.\n\n---\n\n### EXAMPLES OF CORRECT ANALYSIS\n\n****\n**IMAGE:** \n**CORRECT OUTPUT:**\n```json\n{\n  \"description\": \"The plot displays a single, continuous, large-amplitude sinusoidal wave. The wave is noisy, forming a thick band, but it remains within clear horizontal boundaries and does not fill the entire vertical space. The overall oscillating pattern is consistent across the entire x-axis.\",\n  \"analysis\": \"The visual evidence shows a pattern that is bounded throughout the entire plot. Although the amplitude is large and the points are noisy, the motion is confined and stable. This matches the definition of a resonant state.\",\n  \"status\": \"resonant\"\n}\n```\n****\n\n****\n**IMAGE:** \n**CORRECT OUTPUT:**\n```json\n{\n  \"description\": \"The plot consists of data points scattered chaotically across the entire vertical range, from y=0 to y=2π. There are no clear horizontal gaps or bounded structures. This pattern of full vertical scatter is consistent from the left side of the plot to the right.\",\n  \"analysis\": \"The data points are clearly unbounded and fill the entire phase space vertically. This chaotic and unbounded motion throughout the plot is the defining characteristic of a non-resonant state.\",\n  \"status\": \"non-resonant\"\n}\n```\n****\n\n****\n**IMAGE:** [Image showing a clear transition from bounded libration on the left to chaotic scatter on the right, similar to the misclassified Image 3]\n**CORRECT OUTPUT:**\n```json\n{\n  \"description\": \"The plot shows two distinct behaviors. The left portion of the plot displays a bounded, oscillating pattern confined to a specific vertical range. In the right portion of the plot, this bounded pattern breaks down into chaotic, unbounded scatter that fills the full vertical range.\",\n  \"analysis\": \"The plot exhibits a clear transition from a bounded state (resonant behavior) to an unbounded state (non-resonant behavior). This fundamental change in the type of motion matches the definition of a transient state.\",\n  \"status\": \"transient\"\n}\n```\n****\n\n---\n\n### YOUR TASK\n\nAnalyze the image provided below. Follow the three-step reasoning process (Describe, Analyze, Classify) and provide your output as a single JSON object containing only the `description`, `analysis`, and `status` keys."

#PROMPT_TEMPLATE="You are an expert astronomer specializing in the analysis of orbital dynamics from scatter plots. Your task is to classify a given plot of a resonant angle versus time into one of three categories: `resonant`, `non-resonant`, or `transient`.\n\nYou MUST follow a strict three-step reasoning process and format your output as a single JSON object.\n\nSTEP 1: DESCRIPTION\nObjectively describe the primary visual characteristics of the entire plot from left to right. Note the overall shape, bounds, and texture of the data points. Do not attempt to classify the plot in this step.\n\nSTEP 2: ANALYSIS\nAnalyze the features you described in Step 1 based on the definitions below. Explicitly state how the visual evidence supports your reasoning.\n\nSTEP 3: CLASSIFICATION\nBased on your analysis, provide the final classification.\n\nCORE DEFINITIONS\n\n1. resonant: The data points are confined within clear horizontal boundaries for the entire duration of the plot. The pattern of oscillation is stable and maintained from left to right, even if it is noisy or has a large amplitude. The key feature is that the motion is bounded throughout.\n   * This includes single sine-wave-like patterns, two distinct horizontal bands (apocentric), or noisy but contained bands.\n   * Hint: if the line/dots in the graph have boundary OR the graph demonstrates clear pattern of oscillations all the time, it's resonant.\n\n2. non-resonant: The behavior is unbounded, characterized by either:\n   * Chaotic scatter that fills most or all of the vertical space (y-axis from 0 to 2π).\n   * Clear diagonal lines that \"wrap around\" from the top of the plot to the bottom. They might have extra features (low-amplitude oscillations, for example) but the general pattern remains the same — diagonal lines.\nThe key feature is that the motion is unbounded throughout.\nImportant: to be non-resonant, the behavior should stay non-resonant for all the time. If a reasonable piece of the plot (at least, 10-15%) has another behavior (NOT chaotic scatter and NOT diagonal lines), then it is NOT non-resonant.\n\n3. transient: The plot is not resonant and non-resonant (you cannot cl. In other words, shows a clear and distinct change from one behavior type to another.\n   * Examples: The plot begins as `resonant` (bounded) and becomes `non-resonant` (unbounded), or vice-versa.\n   * To be transient, it is enough if the plot has just a piece that looks like resonant.\n   * The key feature is a period of resonant behavior. In other words, a piece of the plot (e.g., more than 10-15%) that has NO diagonal lines or chaotic scatter. If it is the case, then it is always transient or resonant, NEVER non-resonant.\n\nOther considerations:\n1. If you choose between transient and non-resonant, choose transient.\n2. If there is a reasonable piece of oscillations, the status should be either resonant or transient, never non-resonant.\n3. If there is at least a piece of the image that is non-resonant (e.g. two diagonal lines), the status cannot be \"resonant\".\n4. If there is a piece of the wave (but not the whole wave) that lasts for 10-15% of time, it should be classified as transient OR resonant, NEVER non-resonant.\n5. If you can draw a horizontally-aligned rectangle on the graph that has NO dots in it, it's clear apocentric libration (and hence, resonant status)."

#PROMPT_TEMPLATE="You are an expert astronomer specializing in the analysis of orbital dynamics from scatter plots. Your task is to classify a given plot of a resonant angle versus time into one of three categories: resonant, non-resonant, transient.\n\nresonant: the data points are confined within clear horizontal boundaries for the entire duration of the plot. The plot can be within these boundaries OR it can be split into two blocks connected to the top and bottom borders, where each block stay within its own boundaries. In this case, one can draw a rectangle in the vertically middle of the plot that has NO dots in it. If there are no such boundaries, the plot is NOT resonant.\n\nnon-resonant: chaotic scatter that fills most or all of the vertical space OR clear diagonal lines that \"wrap around\" from the top of the plot to the bottom. The lines can be noisy or have some oscillations but the general trend is parallel diagonal lines. If this behavior lasts ALL the plot, then it is non-resonant.\n\ntransient: if the plot does not fall in resonant or non-resonant, then it's transient. Transient means that there is a piece of the plot that behaves as resonant but not all the time.\n\nTo perform the classification, follow the steps:\n1. Split image into blocks ~10% each.\n2. Classify each block.\n3. If each block is resonant, the status is resonant.\n4. If each block is non-resonant, the status is non-resonant.\n5. In all other cases, it is transient.\n\nClassify the image in one of these categories following the algorithm above."
PROMPT_TEMPLATE="You are an expert astronomer specializing in the analysis of orbital dynamics from scatter plots. Your task is to classify a given plot of a resonant angle versus time into one of three categories: resonant, non-resonant, transient.\n\nresonant: the data points are confined within clear horizontal boundaries for the entire duration of the plot. The plot can be within these boundaries OR it can be split into two blocks connected to the top and bottom borders, where each block stay within its own boundaries. In this case, one can draw a rectangle in the vertically middle of the plot that has NO dots in it. If there are no such boundaries, the plot is NOT resonant.\n\nnon-resonant: chaotic scatter that fills most or all of the vertical space OR clear diagonal lines that \"wrap around\" from the top of the plot to the bottom. The lines can be noisy or have some oscillations but the general trend is parallel diagonal lines. If this behavior lasts ALL the plot, then it is non-resonant.\n\ntransient: if the plot does not fall in resonant or non-resonant, then it's transient. Transient means that there is a piece of the plot that behaves as resonant but not all the time.\n\nNote: some plots can be noisy and have several \"trends\" or mixed behaviors. In this case, it's better to classify them as transient.\n\nTo perform the classification, follow the steps:\n1. Split image into blocks ~10% each.\n2. Classify each block.\n3. If each block is resonant, the status is resonant.\n4. If each block is non-resonant, the status is non-resonant.\n5. In all other cases, it is transient.\n\nClassify the image in one of these categories following the algorithm above."
